<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark.</span>
The offline benchmark is built from 31 consecutive days of search logs on an industrial e-commerce platform. From the first 30 days we randomly sample <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\!\times\!10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\!\times\!10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> user request logs with interactions as the training set, and from day 31 we draw a test set of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29{,}964" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29,964</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29{,}964</annotation></semantics></math> click and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29{,}953" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29,953</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29{,}953</annotation></semantics></math> order samples. After deduplication across the training and test sets together with the recent search history of each user, the query side totals <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{,}629{,}195" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7,629,195</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{,}629{,}195</annotation></semantics></math> queries, and the item side, comprising every target item together with every item in the historical interaction sequence (clicks and orders), totals <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20{,}165{,}617" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20,165,617</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20{,}165{,}617</annotation></semantics></math> items. The typed key attribute words used for codebook construction amount to about <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.08\!\times\!10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.08\!\times\!10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> after deduplication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span>
We compare against two groups of baselines. The traditional group contains BM25 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson and Zaragoza, 2009</a>)</cite>, docT5query <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nogueira and Lin, 2019</a>)</cite>, and DPR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karpukhin et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and the generative group contains TIGER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajput et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, DSI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tay et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, LTRGR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, LC-Rec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and OneSearch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. OneSearch is our prior full-pipeline generative system that unifies recall, pre-ranking, and ranking, with a recall stage built on a keyword-enhanced hierarchical quantization. The closed-codebook recall encoding of OneSearch offers essentially no inference-time editability, so on the intervention axis it is bounded by the closed-codebook paradigms (RQ-OPQ) of Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.4. Encoding Paradigm Comparison (RQ3) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, which attain near-zero IHR by construction. All generative baselines in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> share the BART-base backbone, the same training data, and the same materialization of up to five items per SID as OneRetrieval.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span>
Offline we report Hit Rate (HR<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math>) and Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math>) on both the click and order targets at <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\!\in\!\{10,100,350\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>10,100,350</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\!\in\!\{10,100,350\}</annotation></semantics></math>. To quantify real-time intervention capability we introduce two complementary metrics. The Intervention Hit Rate (IHR<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math>) is computed exactly as HR<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math> except that the target is a fabricated item rather than the genuinely interacted one; it maps the generated SIDs back to items and measures the fraction of samples for which the fabricated target, reachable only through a newly injected code, appears among the top-<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. The Intervention Activation Rate (IAR<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math>) is the word-level counterpart of IHR<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math>: rather than requiring a single fabricated item to be recovered, it measures the fraction of intervention queries for which the injected attribute word is activated in at least one of the top-<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> recalled results, which matches the production setting in which an operator binds a word to a target item population rather than to a single item. Because IAR<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math> is defined for any method that can route an injected word, it serves as the metric for comparing OneRetrieval against the editable inverted-index incumbent (Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Editability Against the Editable Incumbent. ‣ 4.4. Encoding Paradigm Comparison (RQ3) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The intervention signal is identical in kind for click and order samples, so for both metrics we pool the two and report a single <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total</em> value, with the probe that produces the fabricated items and their injected codes detailed in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. Encoding Paradigm Comparison (RQ3) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. Online we report Item CTR, Buyer count, and Order volume in out-of-mall search, with statistical significance assessed at the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math> level. To probe the search experience beyond the business metrics, we additionally report a manual side-by-side evaluation along three axes, query-item relevance, item quality, and page good rate, together with a per-industry breakdown of the relative CTR gain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span>
The backbone is BART-base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewis et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, extended with an SID alphabet whose vocabulary size tracks the codebook configuration. Models are trained on H800 GPUs and served on RTX 4090 GPUs, and all offline numbers in the main text use unconstrained beam search with beam size <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>. For each generated SID we materialize up to five items through a precomputed SID-to-item lookup index, and all retrieval metrics are computed at the item level on the resulting list; HR<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@350</annotation></semantics></math>, for example, is the hit rate over the top-<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>350</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">350</annotation></semantics></math> items obtained after the beam-search SIDs are mapped back to items. All four SFT stages use a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and a batch size of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>; Stages 0–2 run for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> epochs and Stage 3 for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math>, over roughly <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math>M, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="74.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>74.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">74.3</annotation></semantics></math>M, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.6</annotation></semantics></math>M, and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.4</annotation></semantics></math>M stage-specific examples, respectively. The per-position <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-means cluster count equals the cluster-block size defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Codebook Construction ‣ 3. The OneRetrieval Framework ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Comparison Against Baselines (RQ1)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the offline retrieval quality of OneRetrieval against the three traditional and five generative baselines.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OneRetrieval Reaches Deep-Recall Parity with the Strongest Generative Baseline.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On retrieval quality alone, OneSearch is the strongest baseline, leading at the shallow and medium cut-offs and on every MRR<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@K</annotation></semantics></math>. This lead follows from the design goal of OneSearch, which is to unify recall, pre-ranking, and ranking into a single cascade optimized end to end for ranking precision: its closed-codebook hierarchical quantization is tuned to place the most relevant item near the top of the list, exactly the behavior that the shallow cut-offs and MRR reward. OneRetrieval targets the recall stage alone, so it trails OneSearch at the shallow cut-offs yet closes the gap as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> grows, until at HR<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@350</annotation></semantics></math> the two are effectively tied: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5482" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5482</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5482</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5550" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5550</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5550</annotation></semantics></math> on order and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6055" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6055</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6055</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6007" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6007</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6007</annotation></semantics></math> on click, the latter favoring OneRetrieval. The two methods stand alone at this depth: the next-best baseline, dense DPR, trails by more than <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> order HR points, and the strongest remaining generative method, LC-Rec, by roughly <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math>. Parity does not reach the top of the list, where the MRR<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@350</annotation></semantics></math> of OneRetrieval (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0880" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0880</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0880</annotation></semantics></math> on order) stays near LC-Rec and far below OneSearch (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1333" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1333</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1333</annotation></semantics></math>); the contribution of OneRetrieval to retrieval quality is therefore deep-list coverage, the property that matters for a recall branch, rather than the precise top placement for which the cascade of OneSearch is optimized.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Decisive Axis Is Editability, Not Retrieval Quality.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because the two leaders are tied at depth, the comparison turns on a second axis that Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> does not measure. OneSearch is a closed-codebook method and therefore carries essentially no real-time intervention capability, on the order of the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0021" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0021</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0021</annotation></semantics></math> total IHR<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@350</annotation></semantics></math> of RQ-OPQ in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. Encoding Paradigm Comparison (RQ3) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, a deficit that closed-codebook generative methods can hardly overcome by construction; OneRetrieval instead recovers the editability of the inverted index. Of the two methods at the deep-recall frontier, OneRetrieval is the one that supplies a capability the other can hardly acquire, and the modest precision gap at the shallow cut-offs is the bounded price paid for it. On the joint objective behind unifying the retrieval stage, deep-recall coverage together with same-day editability, OneRetrieval stands as the more viable choice of the two rather than merely an equally strong one. The configuration evaluated here is L6-D3, justified in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Codebook Design Choices (RQ2) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Codebook Design Choices (RQ2)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The codebook design space decomposes along three orthogonal axes: sequence length <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, per-position capacity allocation, and conditional versus global encoding. We analyze length first, as it dominates autoregressive inference cost. Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Allocation Axis: Capacity Tracks Attribute Density. ‣ 4.3. Codebook Design Choices (RQ2) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the trade-off within the uniform <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\ell}{=}1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\ell}{=}1024</annotation></semantics></math> non-hierarchical family: Order HR<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@350</annotation></semantics></math> peaks at <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!=\!6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!=\!6</annotation></semantics></math> while per-SID latency grows linearly with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, so the two curves cross at <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!=\!6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!=\!6</annotation></semantics></math>, the cost-quality knee. We fix <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!=\!6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!=\!6</annotation></semantics></math> and analyze the remaining axes at that length.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Allocation Axis: Capacity Tracks Attribute Density.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fixing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!=\!6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!=\!6</annotation></semantics></math>, Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Allocation Axis: Capacity Tracks Attribute Density. ‣ 4.3. Codebook Design Choices (RQ2) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> scans five allocations under ECOM6. Order HR<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@350</annotation></semantics></math> rises monotonically with total capacity, but what matters is where capacity is placed, not how much. The three leading ECOM6 groups carry far more attribute vocabulary than the trailing three (Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Information-Theoretic Attribute Group Merging ‣ 3. The OneRetrieval Framework ‣ OneRetrieval: Unifying Multi-Branch E-commerce Retrieval with an Editable Generative Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>a), splitting the positions into a dense head and a light tail, and L6-D3 assigns <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\ell}\!=\!2048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\ell}\!=\!2048</annotation></semantics></math> to exactly the dense head and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\ell}\!=\!1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\ell}\!=\!1024</annotation></semantics></math> to the light tail. The intermediate L6-D1 and L6-D2 under-provision the dense head and fall behind despite spending capacity, while L6-D6 edges past L6-D3 only by adding <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>33</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33\%</annotation></semantics></math> capacity indiscriminately. Because the L6-D1, L6-D2, and L6-D3 differences sit within run-to-run noise, we read L6-D3 as the quality-capacity Pareto knee, the smallest allocation that fully provisions the dense head, rather than the global HR maximum. At <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!=\!6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!=\!6</annotation></semantics></math> it also surpasses the twice-as-long uniform L12 identifier at half the autoregressive cost, so the non-uniform refinement is captured at the already-optimal length rather than compensating for a suboptimal one.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.13533v1/figure/fig_codebook_length_axis.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/235;" width="476" height="235" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Length axis of the codebook design. Order HR<math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="@350" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">@350</annotation></semantics></math> of the uniform <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\ell}{=}1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\ell}{=}1024</annotation></semantics></math> family peaks at <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L{=}6</annotation></semantics></math>, while per-SID autoregressive latency grows linearly with <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>; the two curves cross at <math id="S4.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L{=}6</annotation></semantics></math>. The deployed L6-D3 configuration (gold star) lifts HR above the <math id="S4.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L{=}6</annotation></semantics></math> uniform point through density-aware non-uniform allocation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Per-position capacity allocation at <math id="S4.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L{=}6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L{=}6</annotation></semantics></math> under ECOM6, all non-hierarchical. Total <math id="S4.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is the summed core codebook size over the six positions. The deployed configuration L6-D3 is shaded.</figcaption>
